  "logline": "On Tokyo's first train of the day, a night-shift baker and an insomniac translator keep choosing the same seat across from each other — and build, over one spring of wordless mornings, a relationship neither dares to name, because naming it would mean admitting what the daylight hours have done to them.",
  "tagline": "Some people you only meet at dawn.",
  "synopsis": "The 4:38 from Shinjuku carries Tokyo's invisible workforce: cleaners, bakers, fishermen, the sleepless. Yuna, a translator who has not slept through a night since her marriage ended, rides it to avoid an empty apartment. Across from her, most mornings, sits Daichi, a Ginza baker who leaves a different pastry in the seat pocket on days she looks sad. They do not speak. A whole courtship happens in gestures — an umbrella left behind three times, a book returned with notes in margins neither will admit to writing, the morning he is absent and she rides a stop too far without knowing why. Then his bakery announces a transfer to Osaka. Five dawns remain to decide whether a relationship that has only survived untested by daylight can survive being named.",
