  "logline": "Andes, 1847: mapmaker's daughter Isabel Ferreira, hired to fair-copy her dead father's last survey, finds a city he drew in perfect detail — then inked out of every map in South America, including, she realizes, by her own father's hand.",
  "tagline": "Some places were hidden to protect them.",
  "synopsis": "Andes, 1847. Isabel Ferreira can read a watershed like scripture, but the Royal Geographical Society employs her only to copy other men's maps. Fair-copying her late father's final Amazon survey, she finds beneath his elegant erasures a complete city — terraces, aqueducts, a harbor on a dry river — drawn in his unmistakable hand, then deliberately scraped away. Every archive holds the same shaped absence, as if the continent's mapmakers signed a pact in one decade. Guided by a dismissed scout whose grandfather traded with the city, pursued by the Society agent who burned her father's field books, Isabel follows the one copy hidden where only a mapmaker's daughter would look: encoded in the map's decorative border. What waits at the end of the erased river is not lost gold but a living city that chose deletion — deciding whether her arrival is discovery, or the first invasion.",
