  "logline": "Botanist Miriam Hale inherits a derelict Oregon orchard and a survey showing fourteen graves beneath the trees — but the names on the markers belong to people no record anywhere admits ever lived, and the trees above each grave are a variety that should not exist.",
  "tagline": "Every tree here is planted over someone.",
  "synopsis": "Botanist Miriam Hale inherits Blue Orchard from an aunt she met once: forty acres of abandoned heirloom apples in Oregon's Willamette Valley. The deed's conditions read as eccentricity — never graft the trees, never cut the fourteen oldest, never dig between the rows after dark. Walking the property, she learns why. Between the rows stand fourteen low stones bearing names that exist nowhere else on Earth: no births, no deaths, no census, no descendants. Above each stone grows a variety absent from every pomological record — blue-skinned apples ripening in the order the stones were set. Decoding her aunt's grafting ledgers, kept like ships' logs for ninety years, Miriam understands the orchard is not hiding the unnamed dead. It is maintaining them. The deed is not superstition. It is a schedule — and something in the rows is due to be harvested this autumn.",
