  "synopsis": "The Rex, a 600-seat picture palace in a shrinking Ohio steel town, has been run by the Fenn family since 1938. Its last owner, Claire Fenn, 58, has signed the demolition contract: in three weeks the Rex becomes a parking structure. Stripping the projection booth, she finds a walled-up cache — the missing final reels of 'A Season in Hellfire' (1956), the film whose star, Vivian Marlowe, was destroyed by a scandal the reels suggest was manufactured by the studio to seize the film from its director: Claire's own great-aunt, whose name was removed from it. A young archivist and the 91-year-old who was Marlowe's stand-in help Claire authenticate the reels while the studio's modern successor moves, politely and then not politely, to buy her silence. Claire's choice: sell the past, or project it once, in the room it was hidden for.",
