  "productionType": "LIVE ACTION",
  "productionNote": "The end of the world seen from one television studio is a performance film: real sets, real screens, and actors holding a broadcast — and a country — together live.",
  "logline": "When every network on Earth goes dark in the same hour, one regional TV station stays on air — and its producer discovers the emergency messages she is reading have been subtly, deliberately rewritten.",
  "tagline": "Stay on the air. Trust nothing you read.",
  "synopsis": "At 19:04 one Tuesday, satellite, cable and streaming fail worldwide, simultaneously, without explanation. One signal survives: Meridian 7, a regional station whose old transmitter keeps broadcasting to three states. Producer Dana Reyes, 43, becomes the voice of the emergency, reading bulletins arriving through the one working government line. On the third day she notices the bulletins have changed in her hands: an evacuation order reversed, a water warning softened, a curfew moved an hour earlier — small edits, each deniable, each saving or endangering thousands, none of them hers. Someone with access to the last trusted channel on Earth is using it, patiently, one adjective at a time. With her skeleton crew, a ham-radio network reporting what the bulletins cause on the ground, and authorities coming to seize her transmitter, Reyes must decide what truth is worth when hers is the only voice anyone can hear.",
